sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-22> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Fluid surface protoplasm creeps up the stiff axial filaments of a heliozoan such as Actinophrys, and because this surface-tension pull is symmetrical around each filament the filaments are set normal to the cell surface, radiating from the centre; closely spaced filaments produce honeycomb-like depressions between them.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Symmetrical surface-tension pull sets heliozoan filaments radially";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Heliozoa and radiating filaments”. Scope: Heliozoa and radiolarians with radiating axopodia or spicules"
}
